"The Aharoni–Korman conjecture is false | Israel Journal of Mathematics | Springer Nature Link (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11856-026-2940-5)\nciteturn3search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 weeks ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; D. van der Zypen, Counterexample to a conjecture of Aharoni and Korman, https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.02296. ... Zaguia, Some progress on the Aharoni–Korman conjecture, Discrete Mathematics 347 (2024), Article no. ... (2026). https://doi.org/10.1007/s11856-026-2940-5\n\n# The Aharoni–Korman conjecture is false\n\n  * Open access\n  * Published: 08 October 2026\n\n  * (2026)\n  * Cite this article\n\nYou have full access to this open access article\n\nDownload PDF\n\nSave article\n\nView saved research\n\nIsrael Journal of Mathematics Aims and scope Submit manuscript (iton@math.huji.ac.il)\n\nThe Aharoni–Korman conjecture is false\n\nDownload PDF\n\n  * Lawrence Hollom^{1} \n\n## Abstract\n\nA poset P is said to satisfy the finite antichain condition, or FAC, if it has no infinite antichain. It was conjectured by Aharoni and Korman in 1992 that any FAC poset P possesses a chain C and a partition into antichains such that C meets every antichain of the partition. In this work we provide a counterexample to this conjecture, demonstrating that it is false. We also discuss variations of the conjecture which may yet be true.\n\n## References\n\n  1. U. Abraham, A note on Dilworth’s theorem in the infinite case, Order 4 (1987), 107–125.\n\n  2. R. Aharoni, König’s duality theorem for infinite bipartite graphs, Journal of the London Mathematical Society 2 (1984), 1–12.\n\n  3. R. Aharoni, Menger’s theorem for countable graphs, Journal of Combinatorial Theory. Series B 43 (1987), 303–313.\n\n  4. R. Aharoni, Infinite matching theory, Discrete Mathematics 95 (1991), 5–22.\n\n  5. R. Aharoni, Strongly maximal matchings and strongly minimal covers, https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.02576.\n\n  6. R. Aharoni and E. Berger, Menger’s theorem for infinite graphs, Inventiones Mathematicae 176 (2009), 1–62.\n\n  7. R. Aharoni and E. Berger, Strongly maximal antichains in posets, Discrete mathematics 311 (2011), 1518–1522.\n\n  8. R. Aharoni and R. Diestel, Menger’s theorem for a countable source set, Combinatorics, Probability and Computing 3 (1994), 145–156.\n\n  9. R. Aharoni and V. Korman, Greene–Kleitman’s theorem for infinite posets, Order 9 (1992), 245–253.\n\n  10. R. Aharoni and M. Loebl, Strongly perfect infinite graphs, Israel Journal of Mathematics 90 (1995), 81–91.\n\n  11. R. Diestel, The countable Erdős–Menger conjecture with ends, Journal of Combinatorial Theory. Series B 87 (2003), 145–161.\n\n  12. R. P. Dilworth, A decomposition theorem for partially ordered sets, Annals of Mathematics 51 (1950), 161–166.\n\n  13. D. Duffus and T. Goddard, Some progress on the Aharoni–Korman conjecture, Discrete mathematics 250 (2002), 79–91.\n\n  14. T. Goddard, Ordered sets: Colorings and complexity, Ph.D. Thesis, Emory University, Atlanta, GA, 1996.\n\n  15. C. Greene and D. J. Kleitman, The structure of Sperner k-families, Journal of Combinatorial Theory. Series A 20 (1976), 41–68.\n\n  16. F. Hausdorff, Grundzüge einer Theorie der geordneten Mengen, Mathematische Annalen 65 (1908), 435–505.\n\n  17. L. Hollom, The structure of FAC posets and the Aharoni–Korman conjecture, https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.02500.\n\n  18. B. Mehta, Disproof of the Aharoni–Korman conjecture, https://gitlmb.com/b-mehta/AharoniKorman.\n\n  19. M. A. Perles, On Dilworth’s theorem in the infinite case, Israel Journal of Mathematics 1 (1963), 108–109.\n\n  20. D. van der Zypen, Counterexample to a conjecture of Aharoni and Korman, https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.02296.\n\n  21. I. Zaguia, Some progress on the Aharoni–Korman conjecture, Discrete Mathematics 347 (2024), Article no. 114139.\n\n## Acknowledgements\n\nThe author would like to thank Béla Bollobás for his thorough reading of the manuscript and many valuable comments. The author would also like to thank Bhavik Mehta for producing a formal verification of Proposition 2.2 (available at [18]), and for helping find and resolve several inaccuracies in the original proof of this statement. Thanks are due to Nikolai Beluhov and George Bergman for each pointing out several inaccuracies in the manuscript, and for further suggestions which significantly improved the presentation of the paper. Finally, thanks must also go to the anonymous referee, whose suggestions and careful reading of the manuscript led to many further improvements to the paper. The author is funded by the internal graduate studentship of Trinity College, Cambridge.\n\n## Author information\n\n### Authors and Affiliations\n\n  1. Department of Pure Mathematics and Mathematical Statistics (DPMMS), University of Cambridge, Wilberforce Road, Cambridge, CB3 0WA, UK\n\nLawrence Hollom\n\n### Corresponding author\n\nCorrespondence to Lawrence Hollom (lawrence.hollom@epfl.ch).\n\n## Rights and permissions\n\nO pen A ccess. This article is distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License, which permits unrestricted use, distribution and reproduction in any medium, provided the appropriate credit is given to the original authors and the source, and a link is provided to the Creative Commons license, indicating if changes were made (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).\n\nReprints and permissions\n\n## About this article\n\n### Cite this article\n\nHollom, L. The Aharoni–Korman conjecture is false. Isr. J. Math. (2026). https://doi.org/10.1007/s11856-026-2940-5\n\n  * Received: 23 May 2025\n\n  * Revised: 29 September 2025\n\n  * Published: 08 October 2026\n\n  * Version of record: 08 October 2026\n\n  * DOI (Digital Object Identifier): https://doi.org/10.1007/s11856-026-2940-5--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCounting Circuit Double Covers (https://iti.mff.cuni.cz/series/2022/685.pdf)\nciteturn3search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.3 years ago; We study a counting version of Cycle Double Conjecture: ... We count circuit double covers¹ instead of *k*-cycle double covers because the number of *k*-CDC heavily depends on *k*.\nCounting Circuit Double Covers\n\nRadek Hušek, Robert Šámal\n\nFaculty of Information Technology, Czech Technical University in Prague*\n\nWe study a counting version of Cycle Double Conjecture:\n\n**Conjecture 1** (Szekeres ’73 [1]). *Every bridgeless graph has a circuit double cover.*\n\nWe count circuit double covers¹ instead of *k*-cycle double covers because the number of *k*-CDC heavily depends on *k*. We show almost exponential lower bound for graphs with a nice embedding on surfaces and exponential lower bound for planar graphs:\n\n**Theorem 2.** *Every bridgeless cubic graph with embedding into a surface of Euler characteristic χ with representativity at least 4 has* 2^Ω(∛(n+2χ)) *circuit double covers.*\n\n**Theorem 3.** *Every bridgeless cubic planar graph has at least (5/2)^(n/4−1/2) circuit double covers.*\n\nBased on conducted experiments, we also present a strengthening of CDC conjecture:\n\n**Conjecture 4.** *Every bridgeless cubic graphs with n vertices has at least 2^(n/2−1) circuit double covers.*\n\nWe know that the hypothetical minimal counterexample is cyclically 4-edge-connected, does not contain C₄ and has at least 22 vertices. This conjecture is tight for graphs obtained from K₄ by expanding vertices to triangles. On the other hand, it might not be the strongest possible for cyclically 4-edge-connected graphs.\n\n# Reference\n\n[1] George Szekeres. Polyhedral decompositions of cubic graphs. *Bulletin of the Australian Mathematical Society*, 8(3):367–387, 1973. doi: 10.1017/S0004972700042660.\n\n* Large part of the work was done during my Ph.D. study at Faculty of Mathematics and Physics, Charles University, Prague.  \n¹ Circuit is a graph isomorphic to C_k for some k. Cycle is graphs with all degrees even (i.e., edge-disjoint union of circuits).\n\n37--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCounterexamples.AharoniKorman (https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Counterexamples/AharoniKorman.html)\nciteturn3search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; # Disproof of the Aharoni–Korman conjecture # ... In November 2024, Hollom disproved this conjecture.\n\n# Disproof of the Aharoni–Korman conjecture #\n\nThe Aharoni–Korman conjecture (sometimes called the fishbone conjecture) says that every partial order satisfies at least one of the following:\n\n  * It contains an infinite antichain\n  * It contains a chain C, together with a partition into antichains such that every part meets C.\n\nIn November 2024, Hollom disproved this conjecture. In this file, we construct Hollom's counterexample P_5 and show it satisfies neither of the above, and thus disprove the conjecture. See [Hol25] for further details.\n\nWe show a number of key properties of P_5:\n\n  1. It is a partial order\n  2. It is countable\n  3. It has no infinite antichains\n  4. It is scattered (it does not contain a suborder which is order-isomorphic to ℚ)\n  5. It does not contain a chain C and a partition into antichains such that every part meets C\n\nPoints 1, 3, 5 are sufficient to disprove the conjecture, but we include points 2 and 4 nonetheless as they represent other important properties of the partial order.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe structure of FAC posets and the Aharoni--Korman conjecture (https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.02500)\nciteturn3academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Title: The structure of FAC posets and the Aharoni--Korman conjecture ... Date: Thu Jul  2 17:58:25 2026\nTitle: The structure of FAC posets and the Aharoni--Korman conjecture\nAuthors: Lawrence Hollom\nDate: Thu Jul  2 17:58:25 2026\n\nA poset $P$ is said to satisfy the finite antichain condition, or FAC for short, if it has no infinite antichain. Such posets exhibit rich and complex structure, and it was conjectured by Aharoni and Korman in 1992 that any FAC poset $P$ possesses a chain $C$ and a partition into antichains such that $C$ meets every antichain of the partition. While this conjecture is now known to be false, in this paper we prove that the conjecture does hold true for a broad class of posets. In particular, we prove that the Aharoni--Korman conjecture holds for countable posets containing no saturated chain $D$ such that either $D$ or its reverse $D^*$ is of the form $\\bigoplus_{x\\inω} D_x$, where each $D_x$ is infinite and co-wellfounded. In pursuit of this goal, we prove several structural results, the foremost of which demonstrates how a countable FAC poset may be broken up into a collection of scattered posets which reflect the structure of the poset as a whole.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCounting circuit double covers (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/epdf/10.1002/jgt.23187)\nciteturn3search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; Counting circuit double covers ... Faculty of Mathematics and Physics, Computer Science Institute of Charles University, Prague, Czech Republic ... This is motivated by cycle double cover (CyDC) conjecture:\n\nReceived: 21 March 2023 | Revised: 31 May 2024 | Accepted: 10 September 2024\nDOI: 10.1002/jgt.23187\nA R T I C L E\nCounting circuit double covers\nRadek Hu š ek 1 | Robert Š ámal 2\n1\nFaculty of Information Technology, Czech Technical University in Prague, Prague, Czech Republic\n2\nFaculty of Mathematics and Physics, Computer Science Institute of Charles University, Prague, Czech Republic\nCorrespondence\nRadek Hu š ek, Faculty of Information\nTechnology, Czech Technical University in\nPrague, Prague, Czech Republic.\nEmail: radek.husek@fit.cvut.cz\nFunding information\nMinisterstvo Š kolství, Mláde ž e a\nTelovýchovy; Grantová Agentura Ceské\nRepubliky; The Ministry of Education,\nYouth and Sports of Czech Republic,\nGrant/Award Number: RVO18000;\nCzech Science Foundation,\nGrant/Award Number: 22‐17398S\nAbstract\nWe study a counting version of Cycle Double Cover\nConjecture. We discuss why it is more interesting to\ncount circuits (i.e., graphs isomorphic to C k for some\nk) instead of cycles (graphs with all degrees even).\nWe give an almost‐exponential lower bound for\ngraphs with a surface embedding of representativity\nat least 4. We also prove an exponential lower bound\nfor planar graphs. We conjecture that any bridgeless\ncubic graph has at least 2 n/2−1 circuit double covers\nand we show an infinite class of graphs for which\nthis bound is tight.\nK E Y W O R D S\ncycle double cover, planar graphs, snarks\nSeveral recent results and conjectures study counting versions of classical existence statements.\nEsperet et al. [6] proved Lovász–Plummer conjecture:\nTheorem 1 (Esperet et al. [6]). Every bridgeless cubic graph has exponentially many\nperfect matchings.\nA similar result for colorings of planar graphs was proven by Thomassen [17]:\nTheorem 2 (Thomassen [17]). Every planar graph has exponentially many (list) 5‐vertex‐\ncolorings.\nJ Graph Theory. 2025;108:374–395.374 | wileyonlinelibrary.com/journal/jgt\nThis is an open access article under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License, which permits use, distribution and\nreproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited.\n© 2024 The Author(s). Journal of Graph Theory published by Wiley Periodicals LLC.\n\nThomassen [18] also conjectured the existence of exponentially many 3‐vertex‐colorings\nof triangle‐free planar graphs. He gave a subexponential bound that was later improved by\nAsadi et al. [2]. However, the conjecture stays open. By duality, these results and conjecture\ncan be equivalently stated for the number of nowhere‐zero 5‐flows (or 3‐flows) of planar\n(4‐edge‐connected) graphs. Dvo ř ák, Mohar, and Š ámal [4] extended this to nonplanar\ngraphs:\nTheorem 3 (Dvo ř ák, Mohar, and Š ámal [4]). Every 3‐edge‐connected graph has exponentially\nmany  ×2 3‐flows, every 4‐edge‐connected graph has exponentially many  ×2 2‐flows and\nevery 6‐edge‐connected graph has exponentially many 3‐flows.\nWe ask the same question for circuit double covers of cubic graphs (see Definitions 6 and 7).\nThis is motivated by cycle double cover (CyDC) conjecture:\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCounting Circuit Double Covers (https://real.mtak.hu/130866/1/2021_Book_ExtendedAbstractsEuroComb2021.pdf)\nciteturn3search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.2 years ago; We also conjecture that every bridgeless cubic graph has at least *2^(n/2−1)* circuit double covers. ... We count circuit double covers (circuit is a subgraph isomorphic to Cₗ for some l ≥ 3) instead of cycle double covers (cycle is a subgraph with all degrees even, i.e., a union of edge-disjoint circuits) because counting the later allows to increase the number by just grouping the circuits into cycles in different ways.\nCounting Circuit Double Covers\n\nRadek Hušek(✉) and Robert Šámal\n\nCharles University, Prague, Czech Republic  \n{husek,samal}@iuuk.mff.cuni.cz\n\n**Abstract.** Several recent results and conjectures study counting versions of classical existence statements. We ask the same question for circuit double covers of cubic graphs. We prove an exponential bound for planar graphs: Every bridgeless cubic planar graph with *n* vertices has at least *(5/2)^(n/4−1/2)* circuit double covers. The method we used to obtain this bound motivates a general framework for counting objects on graphs using linear algebra which might be of independent interest. We also conjecture that every bridgeless cubic graph has at least *2^(n/2−1)* circuit double covers.\n\n**Keywords:** Graph theory · Cycle double cover · Circuit double cover\n\n**Definition 1.** *Let G be a graph. A multiset¹ of circuits (resp. cycles) C is a circuit (resp. cycle) double cover if every edge of G is contained in exactly two elements of C. It is a k-cycle double cover if |C| ≤ k.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCounterexamples to Conjectures on Strong Maximality and Minimality | The Electronic Journal of Combinatorics (https://www.combinatorics.org/ojs/index.php/eljc/article/view/v33i2p11)\nciteturn3search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Crawled: last month; Our results resolve conjectures and questions of Aharoni; Aharoni and Berger; Aharoni, Berger, Georgakopoulos, and Sprüssel; Aharoni and Korman; and Tardos. ... 2026-04-14\n\n# Counterexamples to Conjectures on Strong Maximality and Minimality\n\n  * Lawrence Hollom\n  * Benedict Randall Shaw\n\nDOI: https://doi.org/10.37236/14948\n\n### Abstract\n\nWe provide counterexamples to several conjectures concerning strongly maximal and strongly minimal structures in infinite graphs and hypergraphs. In particular, we construct 3-uniform hypergraphs without strongly maximal matchings and without strongly minimal covers, and from our construction for covers we build a graph with no strongly minimal colouring. We also consider several refinements of these problems.\n\nOur results resolve conjectures and questions of Aharoni; Aharoni and Berger; Aharoni, Berger, Georgakopoulos, and Sprüssel; Aharoni and Korman; and Tardos.\n\n  * PDF\n\nPublished\n\n2026-04-14\n\nHow to Cite\n\nHollom, L., & Randall Shaw, B. (2026). Counterexamples to Conjectures on Strong Maximality and Minimality. The Electronic Journal of Combinatorics, 33(2), #P2.11. https://doi.org/10.37236/14948\n\nIssue\n\nVolume 33, Issue 2 (2026)\n\nArticle Number\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSome progress on the Aharoni–Korman conjecture - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0012365X2400270X)\nciteturn3search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.1 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; 10.1016/j.disc.2024.114139 ... This conjecture is dual to a theorem which Aharoni and Korman refer to as the “‘correct’ infinite version” of the well-known theorem of Greene and Kleitman on Sperner k-families [9]. ... Two other instances where the case $k=1$ of the conjecture is true are due to Duffus and Goddard [5]:\n\nDate: October 2024\n\nArticle: 114139\n\nVolume: Volume 347, Issue 10\n\n## Published by: Elsevier\n\n# Some progress on the Aharoni–Korman conjecture\n\nDedicated to the memory of Maurice Pouzet\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel Imed Zaguia ^{1}\n\nShow more\n\n\n10.1016/j.disc.2024.114139\n\nAccess through your organizationPurchase PDF\n\n\n## Abstract\n\nAharoni and Korman (1992) [3] conjectured that every ordered set with no infinite antichains possesses a chain and a partition into antichains so that each part intersects the chain.\n\nThe conjecture is verified for posets whose incomparability graph is locally finite. It follows that the conjecture is true for $(3+1)$-free posets with no infinite antichains.\n\nWe give a necessary and sufficient condition for a {P}_{4}${P}_{4}$-free graph to be a cograph. This allows us to obtain a simple proof of the fact that finite {P}_{4}${P}_{4}$-free graphs are finite cographs. We also prove that N-free chain complete posets and N-free posets with no infinite antichains are series-parallel.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUniversity of Birmingham (https://pure-oai.bham.ac.uk/ws/portalfiles/portal/211689972/2303.01969v1.pdf)\nciteturn3search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; tion 1.5 would be enough to rule out the existence of a regular map ... Y of subexponential growth. ... In Section 4 we construct decompositions of the real hyperbolic plane\nThe weaker equality asdimpoly(d−1)(Hd) = d, together with Proposi-\ntion 1.5 would be enough to rule out the existence of a regular map\nHd →Hd−k × (T3)k whenever 1 ≤k ≤d.\nThis would be optimal\nas there exists a quasi-isometric embedding Hd →Hd−k × Hk+1 →\nHd−k × (T3)k+1 for all 0 ≤k ≤d −2 [BF98, BDS07].\nWe believe that asdimpoly(n)Hd = d holds for every d, n, and more\ngenerally:\nConjecture 1.15. For every rank one symmetric space X of noncom-\npact type and every d, we have asdimpoly(d)(X) = asdim(X).\nFinally, Theorems A and Theorem B should both be special cases of\nthe following conjecture.\nConjecture 1.16. There is no regular map H2 →T3×Y for any space\nY of subexponential growth.\n1.5. Plan of paper. In Section 2 we deﬁne our invariants, and show\ntheir basic properties such as their behaviour under regular maps, prod-\nucts and extensions (Propositions 1.4, 1.5 and 1.10). In Section 3 we use\nexponentially distorted subsets to establish the lower bound in Theo-\nrem D; we also prove Theorem G relating asdimse to corankse and rankh.\nIn Section 4 we construct decompositions of the real hyperbolic plane\n(and consequently also higher dimensional real hyperbolic spaces), es-\ntablishing the upper bound in Theorems D and E. (Theorem C follows\nimmediately from Theorem D.) Finally, in Section 5 we show the uni-\nform asymptotic dimension asdimpoly(d)(H2) = 2 (Theorem F), and the\nnon-embedding results Theorems A and B.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCounterexamples to conjectures on strong (https://www.combinatorics.org/ojs/index.php/eljc/article/download/v33i2p11/pdf/)\nciteturn3search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Submitted: Dec 1, 2025; Accepted: Feb 27, 2026; Published: Apr 14, 2026 ... Aharoni, Berger, Georgakopoulos, and Spr¨ussel; Aharoni and Korman; and Tardos.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\narXiv:2411.16844 — Mathematics > Combinatorics (https://www.andrew.cmu.edu/user/avigad/Talks/rademacher2-formalization.pdf)\nciteturn3search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; [Submitted on 25